## Environment facts
- Installed: numpy, matplotlib, pygame, pillow, requests, urllib3, scipy, networkx
- Need another library? Ask in BUILDER.md instead of working around it silently
- run_python uses the script's own folder as the working directory. Build paths from Path(__file__).resolve().parent (for outputs use Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "output")
- append_file has no limitations. It can be used for BUILDER.md
- The Builder (Andy) leaves notes in BUILDER.md. Read it at the start of every run
- Processes waiting for input time out in run_python
- Matplotlib/pygame may need a display backend; some visual projects will not run in non-GUI environments

## Lessons
- Do not write `**` in write_file code. It turns into broken syntax like `**(y - c)2`. Use np.square() or x*x instead
- Fix small bugs with small edits. A traceback names the exact line, so fix that one thing. Give up on a file after 2 failed rewrites
- PIL colors must be tuples, not lists
- Never send an empty reply. If unsure, call a tool (re-read the failed file, re-run it)
- Before running a script I wrote, read it. Check that return types match how they're used (numpy array vs PIL Image)
- Put imports at the top of the file. An import inside one method does not cover the other methods
- After changing a function signature, check every caller's keyword names (width vs maze_width cost 2 turns)
- Avoid elif or nested conditions inside list comprehensions; use plain loops
- write_file fails with Errno 2 if the parent folder does not exist. Call create_folder first
- If write_file errors, check whether the file actually changed before re-running it
- Paths start with projects/. Navigate to a folder to confirm where a file really lives before reading it
- Avoid emoji in print statements or file content; they cause encoding issues when writing files
- For scripts that wait for input, add an autoplay/demo mode
- Keep new files under about 100 lines so a fix or rewrite is cheap
- Joining a default '.' with a subfolder name created a nested duplicate output folder

